    "lawCode": "Add §1266-j, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S2088\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public authorities law, in\nrelation to one-way or round trip tickets purchased for use on the\nLong Island Rail Road and the Metro-North Commuter Railroad Company\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL: To eliminate the cost of mandated\nsurcharges for the cancellation of one-way or round trip Long Island\nRail Road and Metro-North Commuter Railroad Company tickets within six\nmonths of their original purchase.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS: Creates Section 1266-j of the public\nauthorities law; requiring the Metropolitan Transit Authority to\nextend the validity of one-way or round trip Long Island Railroad and\nMetro-North Railroad tickets to six months from the date of purchase\nand to reimburse one hundred percent (100%) of the purchase price for\nunused one-way or round Long Island Rail Road and Metro-North Commuter\nRailroad Company tickets returned within six months of the date of\npurchase and prohibiting the assessment of any surcharge or processing\nfee for the returned ticket.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: On December 30, 2010, the Metropolitan Transit\nAuthority (MIA) Board adopted a new refund policy for tickets\npurchased for the Long Island Rail Road (LIRR) and Metro-North\nCommuter Railroad Company. The recently instituted policy invalidates\nUnused One Nay and Round Trip tickets within two weeks of the purchase\ndate. For a full refund, any unused tickets must be returned within 30\ndays of the purchase date. Prior to this policy's enactment, LIRR and\nMetro-North tickets had been valid for up to six months.\n\nThis legislation will prolong the use of tickets for a reasonable\namount of time.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:  S. 3778D (2012) - Passed Senate S. 3778B\n(2011) - Passed Senate\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: Unknown.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect on the thirtieth day after\nit shall have become a law.\n\n",
