    "lawCode": "Add §§1266-j & 1266-k, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S2088A              REVISED 5/27/14\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public authorities law, in\nrelation to one-way or round trip tickets purchased for use on the\nLong Island Rail Road and the Metro-North Commuter Railroad Company\n\nPURPOSE:  To ensure the validity of any one-way, round-trip or\nmulti-trip ticket, not purchased for a specific month or unlimited\ntravel, for one year from date of purchase and, additionally, to\nensure full reimbursement for returned tickets without additional\nsurcharges, intended for tickets purchased, for use on the Long Island\nRail Road or the Metro-North Commuter Railroad Company.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one adds a new section 1266-j of the public authorities law;\nrequiring the Metropolitan Transit Authority to extend the validity of\none-way or round trip Long Island Railroad and Metro- North Railroad\ntickets to one year from the date of purchase and to reimburse one\nhundred percent (100%) of the purchase price for unused one-way or\nround Long Island Rail Road and Metro-North Commuter Railroad Company\ntickets returned within six months of the date of purchase and\nprohibiting the assessment of any surcharge or processing fee for the\nreturned ticket. Additionally creates a new section 1266-k; providing\nthat additional train fare purchased after boarding by a passenger\nwith insufficient fare shall be rounded up to the nearest quarter.\n\nSection two sets the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  Frequent train passengers, who preplan and purchase an\nextra ticket for future train rides, run the risk of their investments\nbeing invalid after a short period of time. A one-way ticket is valid\nfor 90 days, however after 90 days the policy in place does not have\nthe option of reimbursement. This legislation provides an extension to\nthe expiration date of purchased tickets to one year and,\nadditionally, it requires full reimbursement of tickets at its\noriginal value without additional fees. Currently there is a\nprocessing fee of $10 per transaction.\n\nThis legislation would provide relief for riders who present an\noff-peak ticket or other ticket which must, in MTA parlance, be\n\"stepped up\". Formerly the customer was charged the difference in the\nfare rounded up to the next dollar. This legislation will round it up\nthe next $.25.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: S.3778-B (2011 - 2012) - Passed Senate.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: Unknown.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect on the thirtieth day after\nit shall have become a law.\n\n",
